Lane Scheduler — Code Review
I read the source files and ran the test suite + sim harness. Findings below; line refs verified.
Critical — fix first
C1. Scheduler has no internal locks; pod-watch and cycle thread race on shared dicts
scheduler.py:344-352 — _queues, _classes, plus UtilizationTracker._events and DeficitTracker._deficit are mutated without any lock. The controller carefully locks _pending/_admitted/_running/_running_ctx (controller.py:190-206), but then calls self.scheduler.submit(job) from pod-watch (controller.py:477) while the cycle thread runs self.scheduler.cycle() (controller.py:592). submit appends into _queues[lane][class_id][student_id]; cycle pops from it. UtilizationTracker.utilization() even rewrites self._events[key] via list-comprehension on every read (scheduler.py:253) — concurrent reads from the snapshot thread (snapshot.py:99-100 calls _scored_candidates) will corrupt this. Web snapshot.py:101-115 reads scheduler._queues directly with no lock.
Fix: add a single Scheduler._lock = threading.RLock() and acquire it in submit, cycle, _scored_candidates, register_class, and the snapshot-side reads.
C2. Watches do not preserve resourceVersion across reconnect — silent event loss
controller.py:269-283, 516-531 — both watch loops do w.stream(list_pod_for_all_namespaces, timeout_seconds=60) and re-enter on Exception. There’s no stored resource_version, and no relist-then-watch on reconnect. After the 60 s timeout or any transient error, every event that happened during the gap is lost — including pod completions, which means _record_completion never fires and ResidencyStats quietly stops learning.
Fix: store the last observed resourceVersion, pass it on reconnect, and re-LIST + reconcile in-memory state if the server returns 410 Gone.
C3. simulate.py is broken
tools/simulate.py:14-15, 30-34, 55-65, 154 — imports Lane.GPU_INTERACTIVE, Lane.GPU_BATCH, LANE_NAMES, Tier, CourseClass, and calls scheduler.register_class(...). After the dynamic-lane refactor, Lane is None until initialise_lanes() is called, and the GPU_INTERACTIVE/GPU_BATCH members don’t exist. python tools/simulate.py crashes at import-time with AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'CPU'. CLAUDE.md still advertises this as the offline test path.
Fix: call initialise_lanes(["xsmall","small","medium","large","xlarge"]) first; replace GPU_INTERACTIVE/GPU_BATCH with real lane members; or delete the file if obsolete.
C4. Unbounded growth in DeficitTracker._deficit and UtilizationTracker._events
scheduler.py:273, 246 — both keyed by (student_id, lane) / (class_id, lane) and never pruned. UtilizationTracker re-lists events on every read but never deletes keys, and DeficitTracker never forgets a student. Across a multi-term deployment with thousands of students, both grow without bound. There is no per-term reset hook anywhere.
Fix: GC keys with no recent activity on CSV reload, or prune entries whose deficit/last-event timestamp is older than the utilization window.
C5. _handle_pod_event ignores DELETED
controller.py:296-305 — only ADDED/MODIFIED are handled. If a Pending or Running pod is deleted while in the queue or running set, the controller learns nothing: the job stays in _queues, in _pending, in _running, and in _running_ctx forever. (Deletion is partially handled via the next MODIFIED to phase=Failed, but force-delete with grace-period=0 skips that.)
Fix: on DELETED, dequeue from the scheduler queue, drop from _pending/_admitted/_running/_running_ctx.
High
H1. Documentation drift — test counts and lane labels
	•	README.md:336 claims “158 tests”; CLAUDE.md says 188; actual is 194 (pytest --collect-only).
	•	manifests.yaml:14-15 header comments tell operators to label nodes dsmlp/gpu-pool=interactive / =batch, but the code reads gpu-class=<xsmall|small|...> (CLAUDE.md, README §GPU Class Routing). Operators following the manifest comments would never get any GPU lanes registered.
H2. Manifest image: <ghcr.io/agt/lane-scheduler:latest>, no probes, no securityContext
manifests.yaml:102, 122-128 — :latest defeats rollback and pinning. No livenessProbe/readinessProbe, so a hung watch thread or wedged scheduler is invisible to Kubernetes. No securityContext (runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem, allowPrivilegeEscalation: false, seccompProfile) — the Dockerfile’s USER nobody is the only defense and is easily overridden by a manifest edit. replicas: 1 with no leader election is acknowledged in a comment but there’s no PDB and no rollback story.
H3. CronJob template is non-functional as written
manifests.yaml:160-168 — the script body only contains commented-out curl and a kubectl create configmap --from-file=courses.csv=/tmp/courses.csv that references a file no one populates. Deploying this as-is silently does nothing every morning. Either make the placeholder fail loudly (exit 1) or remove the body.
H4. cycle() and _admit_pod retry semantics
controller.py:608-640 — _admit_pod patches each admitted pod inline inside the cycle thread; on a non-404 exception (line 638) it re-enqueues the job object, but _pending still contains the pod and the next cycle will pick it up again immediately. No backoff, no per-pod failure counter. A flapping kube-apiserver turns the cycle thread into a hot loop logging every cycle.
Fix: record (pod_uid → next_retry_at, attempts); skip pods whose backoff hasn’t elapsed; give up after N attempts and emit a Warning event.
H5. Patch payload is a JSON-merge patch but called via patch_namespaced_pod without content-type
Not verified in this review but worth checking — patch_namespaced_pod defaults to strategic-merge for core/v1 Pod. Appending to spec.tolerations via strategic merge needs patchMergeKey: "key" semantics, which Pod tolerations don’t have (they merge by full element). Confirm the path actually adds rather than replaces the tolerations array, and add a test that asserts pre-existing tolerations survive.
H6. Web server: no auth, binds 0.0.0.0
web/server.py + manifests — the snapshot exposes queue contents and student namespace counts. No Service, no auth, no TLS. Either bind to 127.0.0.1 and require port-forward, or front it with auth.
H7. epsilon, t_half, mean_pct/std_pct not validated
scheduler.py:312 divides by max(utilization, epsilon) — if a misconfigured operator sets LANE_EPSILON=0, every idle course produces inf scores. t_half is used as a divisor in math.log1p(wait / t_half) (scheduler.py:304); zero or negative crashes. Same for ResidencyProfile (wait_estimator.py).
Fix: validate in SchedulerConfig.__post_init__ / ResidencyProfile.__post_init__.
H8. Snapshot reads aren’t atomic across _pending / _running / scheduler queues
web/snapshot.py:59-115 — three separate with blocks, plus an unlocked read of scheduler._queues. A pod transitioning Pending→Running between blocks shows up in both halves or neither. Acquire all locks in a fixed order, or build the snapshot inside the cycle thread and publish atomically.
Medium
M1. _running_ctx leaks on missed completion
controller.py:381-405 — _record_completion deletes from _running_ctx only if the pod was found in _running. After C5 (missed DELETE) or watch reconnection gaps (C2), entries pile up indefinitely. Add a TTL keyed on start_time.
M2. Pod-UID as job id, but jobs survive in _queues across pod recreation
pod_translator.py:139-169 — UIDs are unique in practice, but the scheduler also keys by (class_id, student_id) in its nested-dict queue (scheduler.py:349). If _dequeue misses a deletion, a recreated pod with a new UID will sit alongside the dead Job in the same student bucket. The deficit accounting won’t notice and will keep accruing.
M3. Batch milestone math freezes after first estimate
event_publisher.py:110-123 — milestones are anchored to the first median estimate. If that first estimate is wildly wrong (e.g., empty residency stats early in the term), all four milestones are scheduled badly and never re-anchored. Either recompute on first dispatch or re-anchor if the estimate doubles.
M4. CSV reload is all-or-nothing but silently accepts a shrunken file
course_registry.py:109-144 — if the registrar export is truncated, the swap succeeds with fewer courses; everything not present falls through to _infer_tier defaults of 200/50 (course_registry.py:62-90), and there is no log line saying “registry shrank from 412 to 7.” Add a sanity check (e.g., “refuse to replace if new size < 50% of old”).
M5. lane_for_gpu_class silently falls back
scheduler.py:111-135 — unknown gpu-class values get mapped to the small lane with a one-time warning. Pods labelled gpu-class=h100 (typo or new hardware) silently land in the wrong lane and consume small-GPU capacity. Recent commit message (“Ignore pods with unknown gpu-class”) suggests intent to reject, but the node side still falls back — verify both sides.
M6. Graceful shutdown doesn’t stop in-flight watches
controller.py:252-263 — stop() sets _stop, but watch threads are blocked inside w.stream(...) for up to 60 s. The main loop also doesn’t .join() any threads. Call w.stop() on each watch handle on SIGTERM; join with a timeout.
M7. No SIGINT handler
controller.py:844-845 (per agent’s read) — only SIGTERM. Local Ctrl-C falls through to KeyboardInterrupt and leaves residency stats unsynced.
Low
	•	Dockerfile pins neither Python patch (python:3.11-slim) nor kubernetes client version. pyproject.toml says kubernetes>=28.1.0 and the install just resolved to 36.0.0 — that’s the version difference range you’d allow in prod.
	•	Dockerfile has two pip installs (kubernetes then --no-deps -e .); a single pip install -e . would respect pyproject.toml and avoid drift.
	•	No HEALTHCHECK in Dockerfile, no /healthz in the web server.
	•	No tests for: concurrent submit/cycle, watch reconnection, malformed pods, DELETE events, CSV truncation, NaN in residency profile, LANE_EPSILON=0.
	•	Logging: pod names logged unsanitized; long namespaces produce noisy lines.
	•	Dispatched list scheduler._dispatched (scheduler.py:354) is appended-to but never trimmed — another slow leak in long-running processes.
Top-5 to act on
	1.	Add Scheduler._lock and protect _queues/_classes/util/deficit/snapshot reads (C1, H8).
	2.	Preserve resourceVersion across watch reconnects and reconcile on 410 (C2).
	3.	Handle pod DELETED events in _handle_pod_event (C5) — also closes the _running_ctx leak (M1).
	4.	Fix or delete tools/simulate.py (C3); reconcile the README/CLAUDE.md/manifest comments drift (H1).
	5.	Harden the Deployment: pinned image tag, liveness/readiness probes, securityContext, and either remove or implement the CronJob body (H2, H3).
Nothing committed — this is review output only. Want me to push fixes for any of these on claude/codebase-review-mwtX9?
